Furthermore, $\mc{Z}_\Lambda^\times$ is a subgroup of $\mc{O}_K^\times$ of full rank. Since $K$ is totally real, it follows from the argument at the end of \cite[Section 2.3]{DhanHayn2023} that there is a constant $\kappa>1$ with the property that, for any $T\ge 1$, there is a unit $u\in\mc{Z}_\Lambda^\times$ satisfying
	\begin{equation}\label{eqn.UnitBds}
		\frac{1}{T^{1/2}}\le |u|\le \frac{\kappa}{T^{1/2}},\quad T^{1/2}\le |\sigma_1(u)|\le \kappa T^{1/2},\quad\text{and}\quad 1\le |\sigma_i(u)|\le \kappa,\quad2\le i\le d.
	\end{equation}
